const gmoCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Selection, the original algorithm', note: 'Every crop is a GMO by ancestry: teosinte → maize (10,000 years of selection), wild cabbage → broccoli/cauliflower/kale (one species, many guesses). Conventional breeding shuffles genes wholesale and waits generations.' },
    { math: '2 · The modern toolkit', note: 'Marker-assisted selection (breed by genotype, not field waits); transgenics (Bt insect resistance, Roundup tolerance — one gene, precisely known); and CRISPR editing (tweaks within the species\' own genome — the biology lesson\'s toolkit, breeding-grade).' },
    { math: '3 · The deployed record', note: 'Bt cotton: insecticide spraying down ~40%+ where adopted; Golden Rice (vitamin A — the humanitarian case); pest-resistant papaya saved Hawaii\'s crop. Also documented: resistant weeds (the IPM lesson) and Monarch-adjacent controversies that improved the regulatory science.' },
    { math: '4 · The governance question', note: 'EU regulates editing like transgenics (near-lockout); others (Argentina, US, UK post-2023) regulate by trait — a live policy fork (the law lesson\'s precautionary debate, varietal). The poor countries most needing climate-resilient varieties face the strictest import regimes.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/advanced/breeding-and-gmos">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Breeding is <strong>the genetics lesson run as agriculture\'s engine</strong> — and the
    technology continuum runs unbroken from selection to CRISPR. The public argument is really
    about corporate control, regulation and trust; the biology argument was settled by a decade
    of evidence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="selection\'s history, the toolkit, the record, the governance fork" widget="equationStep" config={gmoCfg} />

  <h2>The evidence base, honestly weighted</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The consensus:</strong> the US National Academies (2016) review — the largest of its kind — found approved GE crops safe to eat and with environmental benefits alongside documented resistance problems. Same year, >100 Nobel laureates backed Golden Rice. The organised scientific disagreement is small.</li>
    <li><strong>Seed concentration is the real critique:</strong> a handful of firms hold key IP; farmers cannot save patented seed — the economics of the argument, more persuasive to farmers than the biology, and the terminator-gene debate (never commercialised) marked its political line.</li>
    <li><strong>Editing changes the politics:</strong> CRISPR varieties indistinguishable from natural mutants force the definitional question — is regulation about process (how made) or product (what it does)? Science favours product; politics, so far, is split.</li>
    <li><strong>What breeding must deliver next:</strong> heat and drought tolerance, nitrogen efficiency, disease resistance for a 2 °C world — the climate lesson\'s adaptation chapter, written in genomes (the coming lesson).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> National Academies, Genetically Engineered Crops (2016); Klümper & Qaim meta-analysis (2014); the biology ladder\'s CRISPR lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
